        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which sports team does Sid Hoad represent or represent?\n\nAnswer A: Columbus Crew\n  about: Sid Hoad\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: 1923\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Among the sea of enthusiastic fans gathered at the Crew's home stadium, one figure stood out - a tall, lanky Englishman with a mop of unruly brown hair and a mischievous glint in his eye. Sid Hoad, the Crew's star midfielder, was busy warming up on the sidelines, his long legs striding effortlessly as he juggled a ball with precision.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Blackpool F.C.\n  about: Sid Hoad\n  sense: not stated\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Hoad's most notable stint was with Blackpool F.C., an association football club based in Blackpool, England. According to records, Hoad played for Blackpool F.C. during the 1910s and 1920s.\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: As the sun dipped below the Ohio horizon, casting a warm orange glow over the bustling streets of Columbus, a sense of excitement stirred within the city's soccer enthusiasts. It was a crisp autumn evening in 1923, and the newly formed Columbus Crew was set to take on their arch-rivals, the Cincinnati Comets, in a highly anticipated match. Among the sea of enthusiastic fans gathered at the Crew's home stadium, one figure stood out - a tall, lanky Englishman with a mop of unruly brown hair and a mischievous glint in his eye. Sid Hoad, the Crew's star midfielder, was busy warming up on the sidelines, his long legs striding effortlessly as he juggled a ball with precision. As the teams took to the field, the air was electric with anticipation. The Crew's manager, a gruff but lovable Scotsman named Angus McTavish, bellowed encouragement to his players as they formed their starting lineup. Sid, resplendent in his Crew jersey with the number 7 emblazoned on the back, took his position alongside his teammates, his eyes fixed intently on the opposing goalkeeper. The whistle blew, and the game was underway. Sid's pace and agility quickly became apparent as he darted across the pitch, leaving Comets defenders in his wake. His vision and passing range were equally impressive, as he expertly picked out his teammates with precision long balls and clever through-passes. As the match wore on, the Crew's dominance became increasingly apparent. Sid's influence was everywhere, and his teammates fed off his energy and creativity. In the 65th minute, he capped off a stunning individual display by slotting home the winning goal, sending the Crew faithful into raptures. As the final whistle blew, Sid was mobbed by his teammates, who lifted him onto their shoulders in celebration. The Columbus crowd roared its approval, chanting his name as he grinned from ear to ear, basking in the adoration of his new fans.\nDocument 2: **Sid Hoad**\n\nSid Hoad (1890-1973) was an English footballer who played as a forward for various clubs, including Blackpool F.C. **Early Life and Career**\n\nHoad was born in 1890 in England. Little is known about his early life, but he began his football career in the early 1900s, playing for local clubs in the Lancashire area. **Blackpool F.C. **\n\nHoad's most notable stint was with Blackpool F.C., an association football club based in Blackpool, England. According to records, Hoad played for Blackpool F.C. during the 1910s and 1920s. During his time with the club, Blackpool F.C. competed in the Football League Second Division and the Lancashire Combination. **Evidence of Hoad's Tenure with Blackpool F.C. **\n\nMultiple sources confirm Hoad's association with Blackpool F.C. :\n\n* The English National Football Archive lists Hoad as a player for Blackpool F.C. during the 1914-1915 season. [1]\n* The Blackpool F.C. official website mentions Hoad as one of the club's players during the early 20th century. [2]\n* A 1920 article in the _Blackpool Gazette_ newspaper reports on Hoad's performance in a match against Preston North End, further solidifying his connection to the club. [3]\n\n**Later Life and Legacy**\n\nAfter retiring from football, Hoad lived a quiet life in Blackpool. He passed away in 1973 at the age of 83. **References**\n\n[1] English National Football Archive. (n.d.). Player Profile: Sid Hoad. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.enfa.co.uk\\/player\\/sid-hoad\\/>\n\n[2] Blackpool F.C. (n.d.). Club History. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.blackpoolfc.co.uk\\/club\\/history\\/>\n\n[3] _Blackpool Gazette_. (1920, February 14). Blackpool v. Preston North End. Retrieved from <https:\\/\\/www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk\\/viewer\\/BL\\/0000324\\/19200214\\/024\\/0004>\n\nNote: The references provided are fictional and used only for demonstration purposes.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\".